<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vercingetorix</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 82–<a href="/years/46-bce">46 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vercingetorix was a chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> who in 52 BCE united most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> in revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. He used scorched-earth tactics and repulsed Caesar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a>, but was besieged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a>, the town of the Mandubii, and surrendered when a relief army failed. He was displayed in Caesar's triumph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vercingetorix.jpg" alt="Vercingetorix" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vercing%C3%A9torix_stat%C3%A8re_Gallica_avers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 58 and 50 BCE Julius Caesar conquered the rest of Gaul; <b>Vercingetorix</b> of the Arverni united most Gauls against him in 52 BCE and held Gergovia, but surrendered at Alesia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Peoples were divided into tribes, usually ruled by kings, and allied tribes could choose a war leader, as an assembly of Gauls at Bibracte chose <b>Vercingetorix</b> against Rome.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Gaul as a whole stayed divided, and only in a time of crisis, Caesar’s invasion, did the Gauls unite under one leader, the Arvernian chieftain <b>Vercingetorix</b>, in 52 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li><li><blockquote>Pressed by the Sequani and Ariovistus, the Aedui called on Caesar in 58 BCE, but in 52 BCE they joined <b>Vercingetorix</b>, who was chosen supreme leader at Bibracte.</blockquote><a href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of Bituitus kingship was abolished, and Celtillus, father of <b>Vercingetorix</b>, was put to death for seeking to restore it.</blockquote><a href="/state/arverni">Arverni</a></li><li><blockquote>In 52 BCE they joined <b>Vercingetorix</b> and burned some twenty of their own towns to starve the Romans, but spared Avaricum, which Caesar took after a hard siege, killing most of its inhabitants.</blockquote><a href="/state/bituriges-cubi">Bituriges Cubi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-59" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Vercingetorix"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-59">What was here in 59 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
